WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.118 --> 00:00:03.816
[screaming]
[bear roars]

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:07.630
Really glad that the
wild is not like that,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.010
because I would be dead
a thousand times over.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.660
My name is Laura Zerra.
I'm a survival specialist,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.970
and I've been practicing and
teaching wilderness survival

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.420
for over 17 years.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.618
I participated in five seasons

00:00:19.618 --> 00:00:21.470
of the Discovery show "Naked and Afraid."

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.390
Today, I'll be looking at
wilderness survival scenes

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.390
in movies and judging how real they are.

00:00:27.162 --> 00:00:29.662
[yelling]

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.300
Unexpected falls and falls in general

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.340
are actually one of the
biggest ways people die

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:38.460
when they're in the backcountry.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.390
It doesn't always have to be a cliff face,

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:41.920
it can just be a steep slope.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.562
It's definitely something to look out for.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.880
As far as climbing into a
horse as a means of survival,

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:52.830
it would totally work.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.940
The length of time it would
work for is going to be limited,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.030
and I think the biggest
mistake that he did

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.040
was not taking that gut
pile and cuddling it,

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.630
because there's so much bacteria in guts

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.520
that continue to stay
alive and produce heat

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.100
once the animal's already dead.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.867
So the guts are really where the heat is.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:15.100
There are certain kinds
of clothing, like wool,

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:18.580
that will still keep you
warm and add some heat,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:19.413
even if they're wet.

00:01:19.413 --> 00:01:22.000
I think being naked,
you're already dealing with

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.180
losing heat through being wet

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.910
and any gusts that are
getting in through that hole,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.520
so it seems a little risky

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.520
to take your clothes off
if you don't have to.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:38.158
As he's pulling that gut pile out,

00:01:38.158 --> 00:01:39.810
he realizes it's warm.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.250
And why would you not have it at least

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.230
kind of sealing up the
door that you're in?

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:46.080
I would give this clip a 7.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.380
I think that it's pretty smart.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.700
It takes a lot of guts,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.750
no pun intended, pun intended,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:54.400
to be able to do something like this.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.330
I brought with me only
the bare essentials.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.690
A knife, roll of duct tape,

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.810
in case I need to fashion a shelter.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.760
I actually think someone
on "Naked and Afraid"

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:07.510
once brought a roll of duct
tape as their survival item.

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.744
And they're supposed to
be professionals, so.

00:02:09.744 --> 00:02:11.320
And take a look at this.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.760
I tented my pants.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.370
I've made myself a nice
pants-tent shelter.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.570
I love that clip.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.600
I think it's clearly ridiculous.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.680
And yet, at the same time,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.975
he probably did better

00:02:24.975 --> 00:02:27.420
than a lot of people in
his situation would do.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.000
He was looking at what he had

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.770
and trying to figure out how
he could creatively use it.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:33.810
Now, I think taking your clothes off,

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.270
like, your clothes are already a shelter,

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.510
they're already like your
first form of shelter.

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:40.670
So taking them off to
make a secondary shelter

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.270
probably isn't gonna be great.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.360
But it's better than
running around in circles

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.684
until you're completely
dehydrated and exhausted.

00:02:47.684 --> 00:02:52.680
Michael: Those are
nature's best mushrooms!

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.900
These little buggers
are damn tasty as well.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.690
Eating the mushrooms is a terrible idea.

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.750
Even when mushrooms are edible,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.900
a lot of them in the
wild you have to cook.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.875
So, yeah, he would have died pretty quick

00:03:05.875 --> 00:03:07.740
[laughs] in that situation.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.242
If it wasn't for the mushrooms,

00:03:09.242 --> 00:03:12.270
I would have given Michael's
survival skills a 4,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:15.980
because he's at least thinking
about what he needs to do

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:17.640
and trying to be creative about it.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.833
And that's a great start.

00:03:26.024 --> 00:03:28.582
[wolf growls]

00:03:28.582 --> 00:03:30.410
[laughs] Well, he's in trouble.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.620
But how many times do wolves
actually attack humans?

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.690
And the answer is almost never.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:40.220
I think there's, like, a
couple documented cases

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.620
of that happening in North America.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:44.287
And I see wolves pretty much

00:03:44.287 --> 00:03:45.930
every single time I'm out in the woods.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:47.070
Wolves are terrified of people.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:48.750
If wolves wanted to hunt people,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:49.950
people wouldn't go in the woods,

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.670
because they're amazingly
effective predators.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.810
They outmatch us on every physical front.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.680
They just don't have any interest.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:57.683
We don't look like food.

00:03:57.683 --> 00:04:01.330
If you did run into a
extremely desperate wolf

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.160
who was thinking about the possibility

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:05.160
that you might be a food source,

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.780
then you want to make yourself
look as big as possible.

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.840
Put your arms up, start yelling,

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.400
make some noise, kick some rocks at it.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.360
Wolves can run over 30 miles an hour,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.139
so running is going to do nothing.

00:04:28.651 --> 00:04:29.842
[glass shatters]

00:04:31.427 --> 00:04:33.560
I don't know who else has
ever used electric tape,

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.570
but it's not this rigid stuff.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.167
That stuff's just going to end up

00:04:37.167 --> 00:04:39.610
breaking off further
probably before it penetrates

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.600
the wolf's really thick fur and hide.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.060
And I could see him getting
more hurt from doing that

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.753
than any benefit it would serve.

00:04:46.753 --> 00:04:49.000
Creative. Again, really like his mindset.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.400
He's kind of just willing to do whatever

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.820
and be creative to survive.

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:54.650
I would give this clip a 1,

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.700
because you're already starting
on really false pretenses.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.840
I don't like when this myth
of wolves being man-eaters,

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.248
man-hunters is perpetuated.

00:05:04.676 --> 00:05:05.884
[trap snaps]
Ah!

00:05:07.071 --> 00:05:08.920
Somebody help me!

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:12.430
I'm really super focused at this moment

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.070
on what he used for a mechanism

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.910
to get that much force on that trap,

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:20.650
because that was
impressively fast and strong.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.790
There's a lot that goes into
making a trap like that.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.815
Sharpening a bunch of stakes

00:05:24.815 --> 00:05:27.220
and setting them into another log,

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.320
I mean, we're talking hours.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.340
And then just setting the trap

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.640
and coming up with the right tension

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:36.090
on the mechanism that
you're using to trigger it,

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:37.440
and then finding a place to put it.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.118
A trap only works if you
have someone walk into it.

00:05:40.118 --> 00:05:41.920
So the way you get around that

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:43.660
is either to set a lot of traps,

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.730
which would take a lot of time,

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.150
or by funneling the movement in.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:50.680
So it's not something you
can just do on the fly

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:51.703
and expect to work.

00:05:52.547 --> 00:05:54.001
[yells]

00:05:55.984 --> 00:05:57.363
Don't push it.

00:05:57.363 --> 00:06:01.390
Camouflage is absolutely
useful in survival,

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:03.947
whether it's, you know, in a situation

00:06:03.947 --> 00:06:05.988
where you're clearly
trying to run from someone,

00:06:05.988 --> 00:06:08.010
or maybe you're just hunting.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.600
He had, I think they were
hemlock branches on his back.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:14.870
But unless he's hiding with
his back towards everyone,

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:16.220
it's not going to be useful,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.450
because the idea is to break up the form

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:19.960
so that you don't look like a person.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.767
And I could say the same
about his face camo.

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:24.563
Like, maybe do a better job of, like,

00:06:24.563 --> 00:06:26.828
working with your face
to make it look less like

00:06:26.828 --> 00:06:29.030
your eyes are standing
out, and your eyebrows.

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:31.230
I mean, his eyebrows
were so dominant still.

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:35.960
So creating some kind of
color scheme on his face

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.260
where his eyes and his
eyebrows were less dominant

00:06:39.260 --> 00:06:40.685
would have done better than just, like,

00:06:40.685 --> 00:06:42.280
smearing stuff everywhere.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.145
I would give this a 5.

00:06:45.145 --> 00:06:47.080
Realistically, I struggle with

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.747
the time invested in the trap.

00:07:02.224 --> 00:07:03.690
Some really accurate portrayals

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.730
of survival skills in here.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:08.090
The hand drill is amazing.

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:10.650
Like, spot on, totally would work.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.650
I think the biggest problem I have

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:15.910
is when you're trying to forge,

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:18.610
it's really hard to get a temperature

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.550
that's high enough to forge metal

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:22.240
when you're using wood.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.020
I think wood, a wood fire can burn

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:25.930
at about 900 or so degrees.

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.100
You need about 1,500
to start moving metal.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.100
Otherwise, to get the metal that hot

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.680
seems really unlikely to me.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.070
If I'm in a survival situation
and I don't have a knife,

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:37.940
I will use stone, all the time.

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:39.670
It's obviously ideal if
you have a steel knife,

00:07:39.670 --> 00:07:42.520
but by adapting the
way that you do things,

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.432
a stone knife or stone tools

00:07:44.432 --> 00:07:46.073
can actually accomplish the same tasks.

00:07:46.073 --> 00:07:48.240
Dealing with metal requires
so many specific things

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.198
that stone's going to work

00:07:50.198 --> 00:07:51.823
for whoever you need to kill

00:07:51.823 --> 00:07:53.670
or whatever task you need to accomplish.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.590
I basically just want a
knife that has an edge

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.140
that is going to be able to carve wood.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:02.897
So for traps, for skinning animals,

00:08:02.897 --> 00:08:04.010
having a smaller knife,

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.470
anything where you're carving
or doing fine, intricate work,

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:08.390
something smaller is going to be better.

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:12.660
I would give this clip
probably an 8 out of 10.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:16.890
I think it was really
surprisingly accurate.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.562
The one thing was the fire.

00:08:19.562 --> 00:08:23.062
[screaming]
[bear growls]

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.108
Ugh, another example

00:08:27.108 --> 00:08:29.791
of animals just being out to get you

00:08:29.791 --> 00:08:31.410
in the most dramatic fashion.

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.620
Really glad that the
wild is not like that,

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.990
because I would be dead
a thousand times over.

00:08:35.990 --> 00:08:38.293
Grizzly attacks are definitely more common

00:08:38.293 --> 00:08:40.458
than wolf attacks, and it can happen.

00:08:40.458 --> 00:08:43.820
Usually a grizzly is attacking
in a defensive situation.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:45.950
Either you surprised it,

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.600
there's a mother-and-cub situation.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.270
Basically, you got too close,
and it wants to get you away.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:55.030
It's not going to hunt you
and consume you, per se,

00:08:55.030 --> 00:08:56.640
it's just trying to get you on the ground

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:57.473
so you're not moving,

00:08:57.473 --> 00:08:58.860
and then it's going to
take off and run away.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:02.340
Normally I think it would be
more a case of that happening

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:04.020
when it was a single person walking along

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:06.028
versus, like, a whole group around fire,

00:09:06.028 --> 00:09:08.002
but it can happen.

00:09:08.002 --> 00:09:09.583
[screaming]

00:09:09.583 --> 00:09:10.632
[bear growls]

00:09:10.632 --> 00:09:11.782
Stay back!

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.170
Depending on where the bear is,

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.090
a lot of times they're
pretty used to fire.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:19.360
If they're coming into camp

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:20.770
and they're used to stealing food,

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:22.380
I have a hard time thinking

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.480
that if you have a log

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.100
that's burning with embers at the end

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.410
and you're shoving it into a bear's eye,

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:30.490
that it's not going to at least
slow him down a little bit.

00:09:30.490 --> 00:09:32.010
I wouldn't just wave it in his face.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:34.570
I think that's where they
messed up a little bit.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:36.960
And everyone else, like,
throw some stuff at him.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:38.420
They didn't even make contact.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:40.290
If they're gonna stay
and fight, then fight.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.930
If it was a situation
where you surprise the bear

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.220
and the bear starts attacking

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.690
and it's just trying to get away from you,

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.297
then that's when you want to play dead.

00:09:48.297 --> 00:09:51.410
Stop moving. Don't move
until the bear is long gone.

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:54.513
Try to be in areas where
you can see further than,

00:09:54.513 --> 00:09:57.170
you know, 20 feet, and carry bear spray.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:09:59.000
I would give this scene a 1.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.350
Not saying it doesn't happen,

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:02.375
but this was very unrealistic,

00:10:02.375 --> 00:10:05.600
and the way they responded
was just like, come on.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:07.272
There's better things
you guys could've done.

00:10:10.451 --> 00:10:11.592
Ben?!

00:10:12.476 --> 00:10:13.559
[yells]

00:10:16.865 --> 00:10:19.162
First of all, is that Kate Winslet?

00:10:19.162 --> 00:10:21.110
Which is just funny that she didn't, like,

00:10:21.110 --> 00:10:22.763
find a door this time to float on.

00:10:22.763 --> 00:10:25.000
There's a lot of things I'm
not buying in this clip.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.750
She basically falls through
a perfect hole of ice.

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:29.440
I really struggle with the fact

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.130
that the second she hits the water,

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:33.210
she's just peacefully passed out.

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:34.840
There's no struggle.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.830
There's no hyperventilating.

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:38.860
Like, is she just, like,
one of those fainting goats

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:41.910
who comes into a bad situation
and is no longer conscious?

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:43.480
That's what it felt like watching this.

00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:46.530
She was immediately just out.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:48.997
And it can happen.

00:10:48.997 --> 00:10:52.070
You can pass out, and
you can hyperventilate,

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:53.745
breathe water, whatever, but

00:10:53.745 --> 00:10:55.245
it happened so soon.

00:11:06.063 --> 00:11:07.670
How did he find her in there?

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:09.160
It's obviously deep water,

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:12.300
and she apparently just is comprised

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:14.560
of mostly fat underneath her clothes,

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.170
because she was so buoyant.

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.570
It seemed very improbable for someone

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:20.810
to just float back to the surface

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.090
and not float away to
the sides of the hole

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:24.560
and be able to be grabbed.

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:26.690
He had to run so far to get to that hole.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:28.320
And her body is just right there.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:31.480
It's a little too convenient
for me to be OK with.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:33.950
When he was pulling her
body out of the ice,

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:37.460
it was good that at least his
body was kind of spread out.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.930
His body weight wasn't
just centered on the edge.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.846
I would rate this clip

00:11:42.846 --> 00:11:44.462
probably a 1.

00:11:44.462 --> 00:11:47.319
I just don't understand any of it.

00:11:47.319 --> 00:11:51.327
This is -- this is Vaccinium uliginosum?

00:11:52.652 --> 00:11:54.680
So this is, um.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.110
If I'm trying to learn an area,

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:59.240
I will absolutely bring some kind of book

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.420
to help me identify what I'm looking at.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:04.670
Whether it be plants, animal
tracks, things like that,

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.180
it's super useful to learn.

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:08.680
It's also very limited.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:10.375
You might get one picture

00:12:10.375 --> 00:12:12.733
at one time of the year of a plant,

00:12:12.733 --> 00:12:14.750
so to just have one resource

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.560
and then use it to consume something

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:19.480
that could potentially kill you,

00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:22.543
in my opinion, isn't a great idea.

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.600
Out in the wild, you just
have to be really careful.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:38.180
You are not going to
be consuming something

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.060
that you're not 100%
certain of its identity.

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:42.780
It's a really bad idea.

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:44.410
And, furthermore,

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:47.000
you're probably not
getting that many calories.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.430
It's not going to be the
matter between life or death.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:52.520
You can't really exist off
of the few meager calories

00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:54.060
you're going to get from
a lot of plant foods.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.530
So to run the risk of eating it,

00:12:56.530 --> 00:12:58.080
not knowing 100%,

00:12:58.080 --> 00:12:59.760
it's like a risk-versus-reward thing,

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:01.800
and it loses out every time.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.050
You really can get into
trouble very quickly,

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:05.520
and there are certain things you can do

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:07.690
that will really increase that.

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:09.780
There's very few things
you can actually control,

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:12.165
but what you put in your
body is one of those.

00:13:12.165 --> 00:13:14.400
So don't mess around with anything

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:16.840
that you can't identify 100%.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.380
I would give this clip a 10.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.913
Honestly, it's definitely something

00:13:20.913 --> 00:13:23.362
that can and did happen.

00:13:24.588 --> 00:13:26.795
Hannah: Can you swim?
[fire crackling]

00:13:32.650 --> 00:13:35.280
This is a remarkably unsmoky fire.

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.960
Wildfires can travel really fast.

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:38.690
They can basically travel
as fast as the wind.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:39.620
That being said,

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.060
it really seemed to be
traveling on the ground a lot,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.180
and it didn't look like

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:45.255
there was any brush
there to catch on fire.

00:13:45.255 --> 00:13:48.350
But it can happen. And
fires are super scary,

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:52.070
and being caught in one
would be absolutely terrible.

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:54.490
If you can, getting upwind of that fire,

00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:57.237
trying to figure out the
direction it's moving in

00:13:57.237 --> 00:13:58.670
and kind of move perpendicular to it

00:13:58.670 --> 00:14:00.290
and get out of the path of it,

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:02.870
because the smoke can take you out.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:05.567
Anyone who's ever stood in campfire smoke,

00:14:05.567 --> 00:14:06.750
you know what that's like.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:09.230
A forest fire would clearly be way worse.

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:11.670
You know, carbon monoxide, carbon dioxide,

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:12.790
all these nasty things.

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:15.310
You basically just want to get
out of its path if you can.

00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:17.710
Sometimes that's kind of impossible.

00:14:17.710 --> 00:14:19.820
And there's unfortunately a lot of stories

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.010
where people have been caught
in situations like this

00:14:22.010 --> 00:14:24.002
and didn't make it out,
even highly trained people.

00:14:25.497 --> 00:14:27.756
Listen to me. Take a deep breath,

00:14:27.756 --> 00:14:30.090
hold it, and lay back.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:34.520
I've actually read about people
who've survived wildfires

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:37.520
by being in pools or bodies of water.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:39.980
And kind of the key thing is

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:42.560
that you want it to be deep enough

00:14:42.560 --> 00:14:44.120
that it's going to cover your whole body

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:46.690
and not just immediately get too hot.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:48.680
The flames can actually pass
over the top of the water,

00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:49.830
so if your body's sticking out,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.290
it can definitely be a bad situation.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:53.960
The problem with deep water would be

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:57.960
getting exhausted or just
passing out and drowning.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:00.640
But in certain circumstances,
it could definitely work.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:03.030
I think it really looked
like their only option,

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.703
and people have definitely
done it and survived.

00:15:07.211 --> 00:15:09.107
[takes deep breath]

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:13.630
I think the one thing I
would have done differently

00:15:13.630 --> 00:15:15.700
would be wetting an item of clothing.

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:17.750
And then when I did have
to come up and surface,

00:15:17.750 --> 00:15:19.980
you have a wet piece of
clothing over your face,

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:21.850
so it's cooling down that air.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:23.780
'Cause that air is going
to be super heated.

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:25.170
Like, that goes into your lungs,

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.820
and even if there's not smoke,

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.080
just the superhot air can
burn the inside of your lungs.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:31.588
That cool cloth over your face

00:15:31.588 --> 00:15:33.179
is going to not only protect your face

00:15:33.179 --> 00:15:34.820
from any falling embers,

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:36.970
but it's going to cool down the air

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.420
and just keep your skin cool as well.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:43.960
I would probably give this scene a 7.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:45.548
There's a lot of things with the fire

00:15:45.548 --> 00:15:47.500
I have trouble with, but they did try.

00:15:47.500 --> 00:15:48.825
They put thought into it.

00:15:48.825 --> 00:15:50.780
This could work.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:52.110
It's better than nothing,

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:54.068
but there are ways it
could have been improved.

00:15:58.634 --> 00:16:00.284
[ice cracks]
Irena, no!

00:16:01.764 --> 00:16:02.670
[ice cracks]

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:03.570
Ice is funny.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.640
So, you can't always tell
the thickness of ice.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:07.150
You want it to be at least 4 inches

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:08.760
to support your body weight.

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:11.440
And there's a few factors
that make it more dangerous,

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:13.517
whether it's still or moving water,

00:16:13.517 --> 00:16:15.820
where it is, if it has snow on top,

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:18.060
which can actually
insulate it like a blanket

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:21.040
and prevent it from becoming really thick.

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:23.063
If you look at ice, generally,

00:16:23.063 --> 00:16:25.330
if it's clear, then
it's going to be thick,

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:27.910
but if it's kind of that milky color,

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:30.850
it means it's gone through
processes of thawing and freezing

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:33.620
and probably isn't that thick or safe.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:35.360
And if you do have to go out on it,

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:36.980
you don't want to be running.

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:38.930
What you have is your entire body weight

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:42.230
coming down hard and fast
on one point that's small.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:45.440
So if you had to cross
something, getting flat,

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:47.645
spreading your body weight out,

00:16:47.645 --> 00:16:49.149
there's a million ways
they could have done it.

00:16:49.149 --> 00:16:51.624
[ice cracking]

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:00.870
There's definitely a risk of wet clothing.

00:17:00.870 --> 00:17:03.170
If they were to swim across
and everything got wet

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:05.030
and they couldn't start a fire,

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:06.700
they could be in a bad situation.

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:08.650
Being way back then, I think a lot of it

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:10.160
would have been wool clothing,

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.630
which actually will keep
you warm when it's wet.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.250
It's not ideal to have it be wet,

00:17:14.250 --> 00:17:15.440
you're not going to have as much

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:16.570
of the insulating properties,

00:17:16.570 --> 00:17:19.010
but you'd still be OK if you were moving,

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:20.091
and they could just keep moving

00:17:20.091 --> 00:17:21.785
until their body heat
dried their clothes out.

00:17:21.785 --> 00:17:24.320
People die in a few minutes in cold water.

00:17:24.320 --> 00:17:25.470
It's not from hypothermia.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:28.300
It's from going into
that shock and drowning

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:30.650
or losing mobility in
their limbs and drowning.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.300
I would give the accuracy of this a 1.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:35.840
I think the biggest movie
myth in wilderness survival

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.440
is that it's always
something big and dramatic

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.500
that's coming and trying to get you.

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:41.540
And in real survival,

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:42.470
it's not like that.

00:17:42.470 --> 00:17:43.560
It's the little things.

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:45.690
It's the hypothermia, it's dehydration,

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.650
but it's not as exciting
as a grizzly bear.

00:17:48.650 --> 00:17:51.450
If you enjoyed this video,
